    "title": "Drawdown Recovery Calculator \u2014 How Much Gain to Recover a Loss | CalPro",
    "metaDesc": "Calculate what percentage gain you need to recover from a trading or investment loss. See why losses are not symmetrical and how to protect your capital.",
    "intro": "The Drawdown Recovery Calculator reveals one of the most important and counterintuitive truths in investing: losses and gains are not symmetrical. A 50% loss requires a 100% gain just to break even. A 30% loss requires a 42.9% gain to recover. The bigger the loss, the disproportionately larger the gain needed to recover. Understanding this asymmetry is fundamental to risk management \u2014 it explains why professional traders obsess over limiting losses rather than maximizing gains.",
    "howTo": [
      "Enter your loss or drawdown percentage",
      "Click Calculate to see the exact gain percentage needed to recover",
      "View the chart showing recovery requirements across different loss levels"
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Why do you need more than 100% gain to recover a 50% loss?",
        "a": "Because losses are calculated on a larger starting number than gains. If you have $10,000 and lose 50%, you have $5,000. To get back to $10,000 from $5,000, you need to double your money \u2014 a 100% gain. The math is: recovery needed = loss% / (100% - loss%). This asymmetry gets dramatically worse as losses increase."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a trading drawdown?",
        "a": "A drawdown is the peak-to-trough decline in an account's value during a specific period. If your trading account grew to $15,000 then fell to $10,000, your drawdown is 33.3%. Maximum drawdown is the largest peak-to-trough decline ever experienced. Professional traders monitor drawdown closely \u2014 most have rules to stop trading if drawdown exceeds a certain level."
      },
      {
        "q": "How do professional traders manage drawdowns?",
        "a": "Professionals limit drawdowns through: position sizing (never risking more than 1\u20132% per trade), daily loss limits (stop trading when down 3\u20135% for the day), diversification across uncorrelated strategies, and strict stop losses. Many trading firms automatically suspend a trader's account if they exceed a 10% monthly drawdown."
      },
      {
        "q": "What drawdown level should I be concerned about?",
        "a": "A drawdown of up to 10% is generally recoverable and manageable. 20\u201330% drawdowns require significant attention and strategy review. Drawdowns above 50% are very serious \u2014 requiring 100%+ gains to recover means it could take years to return to your previous high-water mark. This is why cutting losses early is so critical."
      }
    ]
